Given a positive integer n, find its square root. If n is not a perfect square, then return floor of √n.Examples : Input: n = 4Output: 2Explanation: The square root of 4 is 2.Input: n = 11Output: 3Explanation: The square root of 11 lies in between 3 and 4 so floor of the square root is 3.Try It YourselfTable of Content[Naive Approach] Using a loop - O(sqrt(n)) Time and O(1) Space[Expected Approach] Using Binary Search - O(log(n)) Time and O(1) Space [Alternate Approach] Using Built In functions - O(log(n)) Time and O(1) Space[Alternate Approach] Using Formula Used by Pocket Calculators - O(1) Time and O(1) Space[Naive Approach] Using a loop - O(sqrt(n)) Time and O(1) SpaceStart from 1 and square each number until the square exceeds the given number. The last number whose square is less than or equal to n is the answer.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int floorSqrt(int n) {

    // start iteration from 1 until the 
    // square of a number exceeds n
    int res = 1;
    while(res*res <= n){
        res++;
    }

    // return the largest integer whose 
    // square is less than or equal to n
    return res - 1;
}

int main() {
    int n = 11;
    cout << floorSqrt(n);
    return 0;
}


